//@version=6
// ============================================================================
// TradePilot 4H / 15m Fibonacci Strategy   —   tuned for FX on 15m
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A mechanical build of the four-item checklist:
//
//   1. HIGHER TIMEFRAME ALIGNMENT   the 4H dealing range, split at its 50%.
//      Above it is premium and only shorts are allowed; below it is discount
//      and only longs are. Buy low, sell high — everything else is timing.
//   2. LIQUIDITY SWEEP              a major high or low is taken. The side that
//      goes is the side that was holding the resting money, so the trade is
//      back the other way. A WICK through the level is a sweep; a body close
//      through it is a break, and a break is not this setup.
//   3. BREAK OF STRUCTURE + IMBALANCE  price then closes through the opposing
//      structural level, leaving a fair value gap in the leg it travelled.
//   4. 71% RETRACEMENT              a pending limit order at the 71% of that
//      leg, stop at the 100% (the swept extreme), target at the 0%.
//
// The 71% is not a magic level and this script does not treat it as one — it is
// a MEASUREMENT. Entering there puts 29% of the leg behind the stop and 71% in
// front of the target, which is 2.45:1 before costs, and that ratio is the only
// reason the number is 71 rather than 70 or 75.
//
//   risk   = (1 - 0.71) x leg      reward = 0.71 x leg      RR = 2.448
//
// The bonus confluence — the 71% landing inside the imbalance — is detected and
// reported as "A+" on the dashboard, and can be made mandatory.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IS MECHANISED AND WHAT IS NOT
//
// Mechanised faithfully: premium/discount on the higher timeframe, the sweep of
// a confirmed swing (Williams fractals, the free version of the annotations in
// the source), the wick-versus-body-close distinction between a grab and a
// break, the break of structure, the fair value gap, the 71% pending limit with
// its stop and target on the fib extremes, the "still valid until one of those
// two levels is violated" rule, and the fib/FVG confluence.
//
// NOT mechanised: "relatively equal highs/lows" as a named liquidity pool. That
// is a judgement made by eye. The `Liquidity Draw` target option approximates it
// with the nearest unbroken swing beyond the fib extreme, which is usually where
// those equal lows sit — but the script is not claiming to find them, and the
// default target is the fib 0% the source actually uses.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DEFAULTS ARE SET FOR AN FX CFD ON 15m WITH A 4H BIAS.
//
// Every duration is specified in MINUTES and converted to bars internally. Bar
// counts silently mean different things on different charts, and a "20 bar"
// window that is 5 hours on 15m and 20 minutes on 1m is not a setting, it is a
// trap. Drop to 5m or lift to 1H and the timing inputs still mean what they say.
//
// Sizing note carried over from the other strategies in this folder: risk-%
// sizing on an FX pair needs several times equity in notional for a real 1%
// risk, because per-unit risk is tiny. If the order exceeds the tester's margin
// TradingView REJECTS it and you get an entry marker with no trade in the
// report. "Max Position Notional" caps qty instead, and the dashboard flags it.
// ============================================================================

strategy("TradePilot 4H/15m Fibonacci Strategy", "TP FIB",
     overlay                 = true,
     pyramiding              = 0,
     initial_capital         = 10000,
     default_qty_type        = strategy.fixed,
     default_qty_value       = 1,
     commission_type         = strategy.commission.percent,
     commission_value        = 0.0,
     slippage                = 0,
     margin_long             = 0,
     margin_short            = 0,
     process_orders_on_close = true,
     calc_on_every_tick      = true,
     max_boxes_count         = 100,
     max_bars_back           = 1000)

// ============================================================================
// INPUT GROUPS
// ============================================================================
grpHtf   = "1. Higher Timeframe Alignment"
grpSweep = "2. Liquidity Sweep"
grpBos   = "3. Break Of Structure"
grpFvg   = "4. Imbalance (Fair Value Gap)"
grpFib   = "5. Fibonacci Entry"
grpStop  = "6. Stop Loss"
grpTgt   = "7. Take Profit"
grpSize  = "8. Position Sizing"
grpMgmt  = "9. Trade Management"
grpCost  = "10. Costs & Viability"
grpViz   = "Visuals"

// ============================================================================
// INPUTS
// ============================================================================
htfTf     = input.timeframe("240", "Higher Timeframe", group=grpHtf, tooltip="The bias timeframe. 4H against a 15m execution chart is the pairing in the source — roughly 16 execution bars per bias bar, which is enough separation for the bias to mean something and little enough that it still changes inside a week. Must be higher than the chart timeframe or the script blocks itself.")
htfMode   = input.string("Swing Range", "Range Source", options=["Swing Range", "Lookback Range"], group=grpHtf, tooltip="Swing Range: the last confirmed 4H swing high and swing low — the dealing range as a chartist draws it, and what the source's indicator is doing. Lookback Range: the highest high and lowest low of the last N 4H bars, which is cruder but never goes stale in a trend that has stopped making swings.")
htfPiv    = input.int(3, "HTF Swing Strength", minval=1, maxval=10, group=grpHtf, tooltip="Bars either side of a 4H pivot for it to count as a swing. Higher = fewer, larger ranges, and a bias that flips less often.")
htfLook   = input.int(20, "HTF Lookback Bars", minval=2, group=grpHtf, tooltip="Used when Range Source is Lookback Range. 20 x 4H is a bit over three days.")
htfNeutral= input.float(0.0, "Neutral Band Around 50% (%)", minval=0.0, maxval=40.0, step=2.0, group=grpHtf, tooltip="Width of a no-trade band centred on the 50%. At 10 the band is 45%-55% of the range and neither direction is allowed inside it. 0 means every price is either premium or discount, which is the source's own reading — the band exists because equilibrium is exactly where the range is least informative.")
htfOutside= input.string("Block", "When Price Is Outside The Range", options=["Block", "Follow Breakout", "Fade (premium/discount)"], group=grpHtf, tooltip="Premium and discount only mean something when price is INSIDE the range, and in a trend the swing range goes stale — no new higher timeframe pivot confirms for hours, so price can sit a full range width above the old high while the script still reads 'premium, sells only'. That is trend-fighting, which is the one thing this filter exists to prevent. Block: no trades on a reading known to be invalid, which is the honest answer and the default. Follow Breakout: above the range = longs only, below = shorts only, i.e. trade the higher timeframe direction instead of the higher timeframe range. Fade: the old behaviour, kept so you can measure what it costs. Lookback Range as the Range Source avoids the situation entirely, because price is inside it by construction.")
htfHard   = input.bool(true, "Cancel Working Order If Bias Flips", group=grpHtf, tooltip="A pending limit whose higher timeframe reason has gone is a naked bet on a level. On, the order is pulled. Off, only new orders are gated and anything already resting stays.")

pivLen     = input.int(2, "Structure Swing Strength", minval=1, maxval=10, group=grpSweep, tooltip="Bars either side of a swing on the EXECUTION chart. 2 is the Williams fractal the source's arrows are drawn from. Raising it means fewer, more major highs and lows — fewer sweeps, and the ones that come are more significant.")
sweepDepth = input.float(0.0, "Minimum Sweep Depth (x ATR)", minval=0.0, step=0.05, group=grpSweep, tooltip="How far past the swing price must trade before it counts as a sweep. 0 = any tick through, which is the literal definition. Raise it if single ticks through the level keep arming setups that were never a run on stops.")
needReject = input.bool(true, "Sweep Must Close Back Inside The Level", group=grpSweep, tooltip="ON is the source's own distinction: a WICK through the high is a liquidity grab, a candle BODY closing through it is a break of structure and the setup is void. This is the single most faithful filter in the script. Turning it off lets momentum breaks arm short setups, which is the opposite trade.")
killOnClose= input.bool(true, "Kill Setup If A Later Bar Closes Beyond The Swept Level", group=grpSweep, tooltip="Applies only between the sweep and the break of structure. If price closes back beyond the level it just wicked through, the grab failed and became a break. After the break of structure this stops being checked, because a deep retracement toward the 71% can legitimately trade back through the old level.")

bosRef     = input.string("Structure At Sweep", "Structure To Break", options=["Structure At Sweep", "Most Recent Structure"], group=grpBos, tooltip="Which opposing swing has to be violated. Structure At Sweep freezes the level that existed when the liquidity was taken — the low that was there before the move. Most Recent Structure re-reads it every bar, so a fresh swing formed during the drop becomes the level instead; that is stricter and fires later.")
bosMaxMin  = input.int(300, "Max Minutes From Sweep To Break", minval=1, group=grpBos, tooltip="How long a sweep stays relevant while waiting for the break of structure. 300 minutes is 20 bars on 15m. A sweep that has not produced a break in five hours has been absorbed.")
bosBodyAtr = input.float(0.0, "Min Break Body (x ATR)", minval=0.0, step=0.1, group=grpBos, tooltip="Optional. Demands the breaking candle have a body of at least this multiple of ATR, which filters the drift-through breaks that leave no imbalance behind. 0 = any close through counts.")

needFvg    = input.bool(true, "Require An Imbalance In The Leg", group=grpFvg, tooltip="The checklist item is 'break of structure PLUS imbalance', so this is on by default. An imbalance is the three-candle gap: for a down leg, candle 1's low above candle 3's high. It is the reaction point the retracement is expected to respect.")
needAlign  = input.bool(false, "Require The 71% To Sit Inside An Imbalance (A+)", group=grpFvg, tooltip="The hidden confluence. When the retracement level lands inside the gap, the entry sits where price is most likely to react, which is what keeps drawdown small. Off by default because it is a strong filter and cuts the trade count hard — the dashboard reports A+ either way, so run it off first and see how many of your trades had it.")
fvgMinAtr  = input.float(0.0, "Min Gap Size (x ATR)", minval=0.0, step=0.05, group=grpFvg, tooltip="Ignore imbalances thinner than this. A one-tick gap technically qualifies and means nothing.")

retrPct    = input.float(71.0, "Retracement Entry (%)", minval=10.0, maxval=95.0, step=1.0, group=grpFib, tooltip="Where in the leg the limit order sits, measured from the impulse extreme back toward the swept extreme. 71 is not a Fibonacci number and is not claimed to be a reaction level — it is chosen for its geometry: 29% of the leg behind the stop and 71% in front of the target is 2.45:1. Lowering it to 50 gives 1:1 and fills far more often; raising it to 79 gives 3.76:1 and fills far less.")
legMode    = input.string("Extend To Newest Extreme", "Fib Leg", options=["Extend To Newest Extreme", "Fix At Break Of Structure"], group=grpFib, tooltip="What happens when price keeps running before the retracement comes. Extend re-anchors the fib to the new extreme, which is what a trader does with the tool in hand — the order and the target follow price down, and risk grows with the leg. Fix is the literal reading of 'the trade is valid until one of those levels is violated': a new extreme violates the 0% and the setup is dead, which is honest but discards a lot of setups where price simply ran further before turning.")
expiryMin  = input.int(720, "Cancel Unfilled Order After (minutes)", minval=1, group=grpFib, tooltip="Wall-clock minutes from the break of structure, converted to bars for whatever chart this is on. 720 is 12 hours — roughly the far side of the next session. A retracement that has not come by then is not coming to this leg.")

atrLen     = input.int(14, "ATR Length", minval=1, group=grpStop)
stopBufAtr = input.float(0.10, "Stop Buffer Beyond Fib 100% (x ATR)", minval=0.0, step=0.05, group=grpStop, tooltip="Added beyond the swept extreme. The extreme is exactly where the stops that were just run were sitting, so a stop resting on it with no buffer is resting on the noisiest price on the chart. It costs RR directly: at 0.10 ATR on a typical leg the 2.45 geometry becomes roughly 2.2.")

tpMode     = input.string("Fib Range Extreme (0%)", "Target", options=["Fib Range Extreme (0%)", "Liquidity Draw", "Fixed RR"], group=grpTgt, tooltip="Fib Range Extreme is the source's target: the far end of the leg, which is what makes the RR arithmetic exact. Liquidity Draw pushes the target to the nearest unbroken swing beyond it — the resting money the move is heading for — which pays more and completes less often. Fixed RR ignores structure and exists for comparison runs.")
fixedRR    = input.float(2.45, "Fixed RR", minval=0.5, step=0.05, group=grpTgt)
minRR      = input.float(1.80, "Minimum RR To Take Trade", minval=0.5, step=0.1, group=grpTgt, tooltip="Setups below this are skipped. Note that with the default target the RR is fixed by the retracement percentage, so this gate is nearly always passive — it exists to catch the cases where the stop buffer eats an unusually short leg, and to do real work when the target is Liquidity Draw. At 71% the ceiling is 2.45, so anything above that rejects every trade.")

sizeMode    = input.string("Risk %", "Sizing", options=["Risk %", "Fixed Qty"], group=grpSize)
riskPct     = input.float(1.0, "Risk % Of Equity", minval=0.01, step=0.25, group=grpSize)
fixedQty    = input.float(1.0, "Fixed Qty", minval=0.0, step=0.1, group=grpSize)
maxLeverage = input.float(10.0, "Max Position Notional (x Equity)", minval=0.1, step=1.0, group=grpSize, tooltip="Caps qty so an order is never larger than this multiple of equity. It is a REJECTION GUARD, not a risk control — risk is already set by Risk % and the stop distance. required notional = (risk% x equity / stop distance) x price. This model's stop is 29% of the leg plus a buffer, so 10-25 pips on 15m FX, which needs roughly 10x equity for a real 1% risk. Watch the dashboard's Last qty row: an orange (capped) means it is biting and your realised risk is below target.")

holdMin    = input.int(0, "Max Hold (minutes, 0 = off)", minval=0, group=grpMgmt, tooltip="Off by default, because the model has no time component — the trade runs to one of the two fib levels. Turn it on if you would rather not carry a 15m idea through a session change.")
coolMin    = input.int(0, "Cooldown After A Loss (minutes)", minval=0, group=grpMgmt, tooltip="Silence after a losing trade, so the same chop that just stopped you out cannot immediately arm the next sweep. 0 = off, which is the source's own behaviour.")
useSess    = input.bool(false, "Restrict Entries To A Session", group=grpMgmt, tooltip="Off by default — the setup is defined by structure, not by the clock. Turn it on to keep orders out of the thin hours where the spread is several times the average and the sweep is the broker's.")
entrySess  = input.session("0800-1700", "Entry Session", group=grpMgmt, tooltip="Used only when the restriction above is on. Applies to order placement; a filled trade is managed to its stop or target regardless.")
tzName     = input.string("America/New_York", "Session Timezone", options=["America/New_York", "Europe/London", "UTC"], group=grpMgmt)
useBE      = input.bool(false, "Move Stop To Breakeven", group=grpMgmt, tooltip="Off by default. The target is a fixed structural level rather than a runner, so scratching at breakeven usually costs more than the drawdown it saves — and this model expects drawdown past the entry, which is the whole reason the 71% is a limit and not a market order.")
beTrigger  = input.float(1.0, "Breakeven Trigger (R)", minval=0.1, step=0.1, group=grpMgmt)

costTicks  = input.float(15.0, "Assumed Round-Turn Cost (ticks)", minval=0.0, step=1.0, group=grpCost, tooltip="Spread plus commission for one round turn, in ticks. On a 5-decimal FX feed 1 pip = 10 ticks, so 15 is about 1.5 pips — a reasonable retail NZDUSD round turn. This feeds the viability gate below ONLY. Actual costs belong in Properties -> Commission and Slippage; putting a cost model in the script as well would double-count them.")
minStopCost= input.float(4.0, "Minimum Stop (x cost)", minval=0.0, step=0.5, group=grpCost, tooltip="Rejects setups whose stop is too small to survive its own costs. This is NOT covered by Minimum RR: with the default target the RR is fixed by the retracement percentage, so the gate scales with ATR and nothing else. Drop to 5m on an FX pair and the arithmetic bites — a 0.29 x leg stop on an 8-pip leg is 2.4 pips, and against a 1.5-pip round turn that is over half the risk per trade, so the geometry says 2.45:1 while the fill says roughly 1.5:1. At 4x the minimum stop here is 6 pips. Set it to 0 to disable and watch the trade count rise and the profit factor fall.")

showFib    = input.bool(true, "Show Fib Levels", group=grpViz)
showFvg    = input.bool(true, "Show Imbalances", group=grpViz)
showHtf    = input.bool(true, "Show 4H Equilibrium", group=grpViz)
showLevels = input.bool(true, "Show Trade Levels", group=grpViz)
showSignal = input.bool(true, "Show Sweep & Break Markers", group=grpViz)
showDash   = input.bool(true, "Show Dashboard", group=grpViz)
dashPos    = input.string("Top Right", "Dashboard Position", options=["Top Right", "Middle Right", "Bottom Right", "Bottom Left"], group=grpViz)

// ============================================================================
// ENVIRONMENT
// ============================================================================
bool  htfHigher = timeframe.in_seconds(htfTf) > timeframe.in_seconds()
float atr       = ta.atr(atrLen)
bool  atrOk     = not na(atr) and atr > 0
bool  envOk     = htfHigher and atrOk

float barMinutes = math.max(timeframe.in_seconds() / 60.0, 0.0167)
int   bosMaxBars = math.max(1, int(math.round(bosMaxMin / barMinutes)))
int   expiryBars = math.max(1, int(math.round(expiryMin / barMinutes)))
int   holdBars   = holdMin <= 0 ? 0 : math.max(1, int(math.round(holdMin / barMinutes)))
int   coolBars   = coolMin <= 0 ? 0 : math.max(1, int(math.round(coolMin / barMinutes)))

// ============================================================================
// HIGHER TIMEFRAME RANGE
// ============================================================================
// One security call, four values. `[1]` with lookahead_on is strictly the last
// CONFIRMED higher timeframe bar — identical on history and in realtime, which
// is what stops the bias from repainting itself onto trades it never allowed.
htfLevels() =>
    float ph = ta.pivothigh(high, htfPiv, htfPiv)
    float pl = ta.pivotlow(low, htfPiv, htfPiv)
    [ta.valuewhen(not na(ph), ph, 0)[1], ta.valuewhen(not na(pl), pl, 0)[1], ta.highest(high, htfLook)[1], ta.lowest(low, htfLook)[1]]

[swingH, swingL, lookH, lookL] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, htfTf, htfLevels(), lookahead=barmerge.lookahead_on)

float htfHi = htfMode == "Swing Range" ? swingH : lookH
float htfLo = htfMode == "Swing Range" ? swingL : lookL
bool  htfOk = not na(htfHi) and not na(htfLo) and htfHi > htfLo
float htfEq = htfOk ? (htfHi + htfLo) / 2.0 : na
float htfPos = htfOk ? (close - htfLo) / (htfHi - htfLo) * 100.0 : na

// nz to 50 rather than leaving na in the comparison: a warming-up range then reads
// as equilibrium, which is "no trade", instead of relying on how na compares.
float htfPct = nz(htfPos, 50.0)

// Premium and discount are only meaningful inside the range. In a trend the swing
// range goes stale — no higher timeframe pivot confirms for hours — and price can
// sit a full range width above the old high while this still reads "premium, sells
// only", which fights the trend the filter was supposed to keep us out of.
bool inRange  = htfOk and htfPct >= 0.0 and htfPct <= 100.0
bool aboveRng = htfOk and htfPct > 100.0
bool premium  = inRange and htfPct > 50.0 + htfNeutral / 2.0
bool discount = inRange and htfPct < 50.0 - htfNeutral / 2.0

bool allowShort = false
bool allowLong  = false

if htfOk
    if inRange
        allowShort := premium
        allowLong  := discount
    else if htfOutside == "Follow Breakout"
        allowShort := not aboveRng
        allowLong  := aboveRng
    else if htfOutside == "Fade (premium/discount)"
        allowShort := aboveRng
        allowLong  := not aboveRng

// ============================================================================
// EXECUTION TIMEFRAME STRUCTURE
// ============================================================================
// Williams fractals. A pivot is only known `pivLen` bars after it printed, which
// is exactly why the sweep of one is a tradable event rather than a guess.
float phv = ta.pivothigh(pivLen, pivLen)
float plv = ta.pivotlow(pivLen, pivLen)

var float lastPH  = na
var float lastPL  = na
var bool  phTaken = false
var bool  plTaken = false

if not na(phv)
    lastPH  := phv
    phTaken := false
if not na(plv)
    lastPL  := plv
    plTaken := false

// Rolling record of swings, used only by the Liquidity Draw target: the resting
// money the move is heading for is the nearest swing nobody has traded through.
var array<float> poolHi = array.new<float>()
var array<float> poolLo = array.new<float>()

if not na(phv)
    array.push(poolHi, phv)
    if array.size(poolHi) > 50
        array.shift(poolHi)
if not na(plv)
    array.push(poolLo, plv)
    if array.size(poolLo) > 50
        array.shift(poolLo)

// Written as two explicit branches rather than one loop over a conditionally
// selected array: the two pools hold different numbers of swings, and a ternary
// that evaluates both array.get() calls would index past the end of the shorter.
drawTarget(bool isShort, float from) =>
    float best = na
    if not na(from)
        if isShort
            int nLo = array.size(poolLo)
            if nLo > 0
                for i = 0 to nLo - 1
                    float v = array.get(poolLo, i)
                    if v < from and (na(best) or v > best)
                        best := v
        else
            int nHi = array.size(poolHi)
            if nHi > 0
                for i = 0 to nHi - 1
                    float v = array.get(poolHi, i)
                    if v > from and (na(best) or v < best)
                        best := v
    best

// ============================================================================
// SETUP STATE
// ============================================================================
// 0 idle | 1 swept, working toward a break of structure | 2 order working | 3 in trade
var int   state    = 0
var int   dir      = 0     // -1 short (buy side swept), +1 long (sell side swept)
var float sweptLvl = na    // the swing that was taken
var int   sweepBar = na
var float legStart = na    // fib 100% — the swept extreme, and where the stop lives
var float legEnd   = na    // fib 0%   — the impulse extreme, and the target
var float bosLvl   = na
var bool  bosDone  = false
var int   bosBar   = na
var int   armBar   = na
var int   coolTill = na

var array<float> gapTop   = array.new<float>()
var array<float> gapBot   = array.new<float>()
var array<box>   gapBoxes = array.new<box>()

clearGaps() =>
    array.clear(gapTop)
    array.clear(gapBot)
    if array.size(gapBoxes) > 0
        for i = 0 to array.size(gapBoxes) - 1
            box.delete(array.get(gapBoxes, i))
    array.clear(gapBoxes)

inGap(float px) =>
    bool hit = false
    int n = array.size(gapTop)
    if n > 0 and not na(px)
        for i = 0 to n - 1
            if px <= array.get(gapTop, i) and px >= array.get(gapBot, i)
                hit := true
    hit

// ============================================================================
// FUNNEL AND TRADE COUNTERS
// ============================================================================
// "Why didn't it trade?" has five possible answers and the Strategy Tester can
// only ever show you the last one. Counting sweeps, then breaks, then orders,
// then fills localises the loss: a big drop at the break stage means the sweeps
// are being absorbed, at the order stage means the bias or imbalance gates are
// doing the rejecting, at the fill stage means 71% is too deep a retracement.
var int nSweep  = 0
var int nBos    = 0
var int nArmed  = 0
var int nFilled = 0
var int nCancel = 0
var int nAplus  = 0
var int nShort  = 0
var int nLong   = 0
var int nTime   = 0

// ============================================================================
// SWEEP
// ============================================================================
float pad = sweepDepth * nz(atr, 0.0)

bool tookHigh = not phTaken and not na(lastPH) and high > lastPH + pad and (not needReject or close < lastPH)
bool tookLow  = not plTaken and not na(lastPL) and low  < lastPL - pad and (not needReject or close > lastPL)

// A bar that takes both sides is read by its own close: a bearish close means the
// buy side was the side that got run.
bool bothSides = tookHigh and tookLow
bool newShort  = tookHigh and (not bothSides or close <= open)
bool newLong   = tookLow  and (not bothSides or close >  open)

if state == 0 and envOk and (newShort or newLong)
    dir      := newShort ? -1 : 1
    sweptLvl := newShort ? lastPH : lastPL
    sweepBar := bar_index
    legStart := newShort ? high : low
    legEnd   := newShort ? low  : high
    bosLvl   := newShort ? lastPL : lastPH
    bosDone  := false
    bosBar   := na
    state    := 1
    nSweep   += 1
    clearGaps()
    if newShort
        phTaken := true
    else
        plTaken := true

// ============================================================================
// LEG MAINTENANCE AND INVALIDATION
// ============================================================================
// Before the break of structure the swept extreme can still deepen — more
// liquidity taken is the same story, told louder. After it, that extreme is the
// stop, and price reaching it means the setup is over whether or not we are in.
bool invalidate = false

if (state == 1 or state == 2) and bar_index > sweepBar
    if not bosDone
        legStart := dir == -1 ? math.max(legStart, high) : math.min(legStart, low)
        legEnd   := dir == -1 ? math.min(legEnd, low)    : math.max(legEnd, high)
        if killOnClose and (dir == -1 ? close > sweptLvl : close < sweptLvl)
            invalidate := true
        if bar_index - sweepBar > bosMaxBars
            invalidate := true
    else
        if (dir == -1 ? high > legStart : low < legStart)
            invalidate := true
        else if (dir == -1 ? low < legEnd : high > legEnd)
            if legMode == "Extend To Newest Extreme"
                legEnd := dir == -1 ? low : high
            else
                invalidate := true

bool expired = bosDone and not na(bosBar) and bar_index - bosBar >= expiryBars

// ============================================================================
// IMBALANCE
// ============================================================================
// Captured as it forms rather than scanned for afterwards. The three-candle gap
// must sit inside the leg, so its first candle cannot predate the sweep.
if (state == 1 or state == 2) and not na(sweepBar) and bar_index - 2 >= sweepBar and not invalidate
    bool gapBear = dir == -1 and low[2] > high
    bool gapBull = dir ==  1 and high[2] < low
    if gapBear or gapBull
        float t = gapBear ? low[2] : low
        float b = gapBear ? high   : high[2]
        if t - b >= fvgMinAtr * nz(atr, 0.0) and array.size(gapTop) < 20
            array.push(gapTop, t)
            array.push(gapBot, b)
            if showFvg
                array.push(gapBoxes, box.new(bar_index - 2, t, bar_index, b, border_color=color.new(color.blue, 60), border_width=1, bgcolor=color.new(color.blue, 88), extend=extend.right))

// ============================================================================
// BREAK OF STRUCTURE
// ============================================================================
float bosRefLvl = bosRef == "Most Recent Structure" ? (dir == -1 ? lastPL : lastPH) : bosLvl
bool  bosBody   = bosBodyAtr <= 0.0 or math.abs(close - open) >= bosBodyAtr * nz(atr, 0.0)
// `bar_index > sweepBar` keeps the sweep and the break off the same candle. A bar
// that wicks a high and closes through the opposing low is a one-bar fib range,
// and a 71% of one candle is not a retracement.
bool  bosNow    = state == 1 and not bosDone and not invalidate and bar_index > sweepBar and not na(bosRefLvl) and (dir == -1 ? close < bosRefLvl : close > bosRefLvl) and bosBody

if bosNow
    bosDone := true
    bosBar  := bar_index
    nBos    += 1

// ============================================================================
// FIB PRICING
// ============================================================================
// entry = legEnd + r x (legStart - legEnd) reads the same in both directions:
// r of the way back from the impulse extreme toward the swept one. The stop sits
// beyond legStart, the target at legEnd, so risk is (1-r) of the leg and reward
// is r of it — the entire reason the number is 71.
float legRange  = na(legStart) or na(legEnd) ? na : math.abs(legStart - legEnd)
float wantEntry = na(legStart) or na(legEnd) ? na : legEnd + (retrPct / 100.0) * (legStart - legEnd)
float rawStop   = na(legStart) ? na : (dir == -1 ? legStart + stopBufAtr * nz(atr, 0.0) : legStart - stopBufAtr * nz(atr, 0.0))
float wantStop  = na(rawStop) or na(wantEntry) ? na : (dir == -1 ? math.max(rawStop, wantEntry + syminfo.mintick) : math.min(rawStop, wantEntry - syminfo.mintick))
float riskDist  = na(wantStop) or na(wantEntry) ? na : math.abs(wantStop - wantEntry)

float drawPx = tpMode == "Liquidity Draw" ? drawTarget(dir == -1, legEnd) : na

float wantTgt = switch tpMode
    "Fixed RR"       => na(wantEntry) or na(riskDist) ? na : (dir == -1 ? wantEntry - fixedRR * riskDist : wantEntry + fixedRR * riskDist)
    "Liquidity Draw" => na(drawPx) ? legEnd : drawPx
    => legEnd

float rewardDist = na(wantTgt) or na(wantEntry) ? na : (dir == -1 ? wantEntry - wantTgt : wantTgt - wantEntry)
float setupRR    = na(riskDist) or riskDist <= 0 or na(rewardDist) ? na : rewardDist / riskDist

bool aPlus = not na(wantEntry) and inGap(wantEntry)

// ============================================================================
// GATES
// ============================================================================
bool biasOk  = dir == -1 ? allowShort : dir == 1 ? allowLong : false
bool fvgOk   = not needFvg or array.size(gapTop) > 0
bool alignOk = not needAlign or aPlus
bool rrOk    = not na(setupRR) and rewardDist > 0 and setupRR >= minRR
// Minimum RR cannot catch this. With the default target the RR is fixed by the
// retracement percentage, so it gates leg size relative to ATR and nothing else —
// and on a 5m FX chart a leg of one ATR is a stop of two pips, which loses half
// its risk to the spread before the idea has been tested.
float costDist = costTicks * syminfo.mintick
// na riskDist reads as OK: there is no setup to judge, and the dashboard should not
// show a warning colour while idle. An order still cannot pass, because qty > 0
// requires a real stop distance.
bool  costOk   = minStopCost <= 0.0 or costDist <= 0.0 or na(riskDist) or riskDist >= minStopCost * costDist
// A sell limit below market is a market order wearing a costume — the
// retracement being waited for has to actually be a retracement.
bool retrOk  = not na(wantEntry) and (dir == -1 ? wantEntry > close : wantEntry < close)
bool coolOk  = na(coolTill) or bar_index >= coolTill
bool sessOk  = not useSess or not na(time(timeframe.period, entrySess, tzName))
bool aliveOk = not invalidate and not expired and (not htfHard or biasOk)

// ============================================================================
// POSITION STATE
// ============================================================================
var float  posStop  = na
var float  posTgt   = na
var int    posDir   = 0
var string orderId  = ""
var bool   beArmed  = false

var float lastRR    = na
var float lastEntry = na
var float lastStop  = na
var float lastTgt   = na
var float lastQty   = na
var bool  lastCap   = false

orderQty(float stopDist) =>
    float baseQty = sizeMode == "Fixed Qty" ? fixedQty : (not na(stopDist) and stopDist > 0 ? (strategy.equity * riskPct / 100.0) / stopDist : 0.0)
    float capQty  = close > 0 ? (strategy.equity * maxLeverage) / close : baseQty
    float q       = math.max(math.min(baseQty, capQty), 0.0)
    [q, baseQty > 0 and q < baseQty * 0.999]

[qty, capped] = orderQty(riskDist)

// ============================================================================
// FILL AND CANCEL MANAGEMENT
// ============================================================================
// `filled` is read from the bar's range, not from position_size. The broker
// emulator fills a limit intrabar but the position is invisible to the script
// until the next bar, so cancelling on position_size == 0 would fire a cancel at
// the same moment the order was filling. `bar_index > armBar` matters for the
// same reason in reverse: an order placed during this bar's calculation is not
// live until the next one, and the breaking candle itself often trades back into
// the 71% it just created.
bool justFilled = false
bool cancelNow  = false

var int fillSeenBar  = na
var int closedAtFill = 0

if state == 2
    bool live    = bar_index > armBar
    bool touched = live and not na(lastEntry) and (posDir == -1 ? high >= lastEntry : low <= lastEntry)
    if strategy.position_size != 0 or touched
        state        := 3
        justFilled   := true
        fillSeenBar  := bar_index
        closedAtFill := strategy.closedtrades
        nFilled      += 1
        if aPlus
            nAplus += 1
    else if not aliveOk
        strategy.cancel(orderId)
        cancelNow := true
        nCancel   += 1
        state     := 0

// Stale-fill guard. Reading a fill from the bar's range is one inference away
// from the emulator's own bookkeeping, and if the two ever disagree — a touch
// that did not cross — the script would sit in "in trade" forever with no
// position to close and no setup allowed to start. Two bars is long enough for a
// real fill to appear in position_size or in a closed trade; past that, release.
if state == 3 and strategy.position_size == 0 and not na(fillSeenBar) and bar_index - fillSeenBar >= 2 and strategy.closedtrades == closedAtFill
    strategy.cancel(orderId)
    cancelNow := true
    nFilled   := math.max(nFilled - 1, 0)
    state     := 0

if state == 1 and (invalidate or expired)
    state := 0
    clearGaps()

if state == 0
    dir     := 0
    bosDone := false
    // Safety net: nothing should be resting on the book with no setup behind it.
    // Fired on the transition into idle rather than on every idle bar, and placed
    // before the order block so it can never cancel an order written after it.
    if strategy.position_size == 0 and (cancelNow or nz(state[1], 0) != 0)
        strategy.cancel_all()

// ============================================================================
// ORDER PLACEMENT
// ============================================================================
// Re-issued every bar while the order works. In Extend mode the leg deepens, so
// the 71%, the target and the size all move with it; strategy.entry with an
// existing ID modifies the resting order rather than adding one.
bool openNew = state == 1 and bosDone and envOk and htfOk and barstate.isconfirmed and strategy.position_size == 0 and not invalidate and not expired and biasOk and fvgOk and alignOk and rrOk and costOk and retrOk and coolOk and sessOk and qty > 0
bool update  = state == 2 and aliveOk and not justFilled and strategy.position_size == 0 and qty > 0 and not na(wantEntry)

if openNew or update
    if dir == -1
        strategy.entry("FIB-S", strategy.short, qty=qty, limit=wantEntry, alert_message="TP FIB short limit")
    else
        strategy.entry("FIB-L", strategy.long, qty=qty, limit=wantEntry, alert_message="TP FIB long limit")
    posDir    := dir
    posStop   := wantStop
    posTgt    := wantTgt
    orderId   := dir == -1 ? "FIB-S" : "FIB-L"
    lastEntry := wantEntry
    lastStop  := wantStop
    lastTgt   := wantTgt
    lastRR    := setupRR
    lastQty   := qty
    lastCap   := capped
    if openNew
        state   := 2
        armBar  := bar_index
        beArmed := false
        nArmed  += 1
        if dir == -1
            nShort += 1
        else
            nLong += 1

// ============================================================================
// EXITS
// ============================================================================
bool  longOpen  = strategy.position_size > 0
bool  shortOpen = strategy.position_size < 0
bool  inPos     = longOpen or shortOpen
int   fillBar   = strategy.opentrades > 0 ? strategy.opentrades.entry_bar_index(0) : -1
int   heldBars  = fillBar < 0 ? 0 : bar_index - fillBar
float fillPx    = strategy.position_avg_price
float rInit     = na(fillPx) or na(posStop) ? na : math.abs(posStop - fillPx)

if useBE and inPos and not na(rInit) and rInit > 0
    if shortOpen and low  <= fillPx - beTrigger * rInit
        beArmed := true
    if longOpen  and high >= fillPx + beTrigger * rInit
        beArmed := true

float effStop = (not beArmed or na(fillPx) or na(posStop)) ? posStop : (posDir == -1 ? math.min(posStop, fillPx) : math.max(posStop, fillPx))

// Keyed on posDir, not on an open position. An exit attached to an entry ID is
// live the instant that entry fills, so placing it alongside the order closes the
// hole a position-gated exit leaves: with orders filling on close,
// strategy.position_size does not report the trade until the next bar.
bool levelsOk = posDir != 0 and not na(posStop) and not na(posTgt) and not cancelNow

if posDir == -1 and levelsOk
    strategy.exit("X FIB-S", from_entry="FIB-S", stop=effStop, limit=posTgt)
if posDir == 1 and levelsOk
    strategy.exit("X FIB-L", from_entry="FIB-L", stop=effStop, limit=posTgt)

if inPos and holdBars > 0 and heldBars >= holdBars
    strategy.close_all(comment="Time stop")
    nTime += 1

// ============================================================================
// RESET
// ============================================================================
// Levels are cleared on a real event — a trade that closed, or an order that was
// cancelled — never on "position_size is currently 0". With process_orders_on_close
// the entry bar still reports 0 and a limit fill is invisible for a further bar,
// so a reset keyed on flatness wipes the stop and target in the gap between the
// order and the fill, and the trade never gets an exit.
var int prevClosed = 0
bool tradeClosed = strategy.closedtrades > prevClosed

if tradeClosed
    if coolBars > 0 and strategy.closedtrades.profit(strategy.closedtrades - 1) < 0
        coolTill := bar_index + coolBars
    prevClosed := strategy.closedtrades

if (tradeClosed and not inPos) or cancelNow
    state    := 0
    dir      := 0
    posDir   := 0
    posStop  := na
    posTgt   := na
    legStart := na
    legEnd   := na
    bosDone  := false
    beArmed  := false
    clearGaps()

// ============================================================================
// VISUALS
// ============================================================================
bool setupLive = state == 1 or state == 2

plot(showHtf and htfOk ? htfEq : na, "4H Equilibrium", color=color.new(color.purple, 30), style=plot.style_linebr, linewidth=1)
plot(showFib and setupLive ? legStart : na, "Fib 100%", color=color.new(color.red, 40), style=plot.style_linebr, linewidth=1)
plot(showFib and setupLive ? legEnd : na, "Fib 0%", color=color.new(color.green, 40), style=plot.style_linebr, linewidth=1)
plot(showFib and setupLive and state == 2 ? lastEntry : na, "Fib 71% (working limit)", color=color.new(color.blue, 0), style=plot.style_linebr, linewidth=1)
plot(showLevels and inPos ? effStop : na, "Stop", color=color.new(color.red, 0), style=plot.style_linebr, linewidth=1)
plot(showLevels and inPos ? posTgt : na, "Target", color=color.new(color.green, 0), style=plot.style_linebr, linewidth=1)

plotshape(showSignal and state == 1 and sweepBar == bar_index and dir == -1, "Buy Side Swept", shape.xcross, location.abovebar, color.new(color.orange, 0), size=size.tiny)
plotshape(showSignal and state == 1 and sweepBar == bar_index and dir ==  1, "Sell Side Swept", shape.xcross, location.belowbar, color.new(color.orange, 0), size=size.tiny)
plotshape(showSignal and bosNow and dir == -1, "BOS Down", shape.triangledown, location.abovebar, color.new(color.red, 0), size=size.tiny)
plotshape(showSignal and bosNow and dir ==  1, "BOS Up", shape.triangleup, location.belowbar, color.new(color.green, 0), size=size.tiny)
plotshape(showSignal and justFilled, "Filled", shape.circle, location.bottom, color.new(color.blue, 0), size=size.tiny)

// ============================================================================
// DASHBOARD
// ============================================================================
dashLoc = switch dashPos
    "Middle Right" => position.middle_right
    "Bottom Right" => position.bottom_right
    "Bottom Left"  => position.bottom_left
    => position.top_right

if showDash and barstate.islast
    // Opaque panel. The cells carry white and silver text, which is invisible on a
    // light-theme chart with a transparent table behind it — the dashboard has to
    // supply its own contrast rather than borrow the chart's.
    var table dash = table.new(dashLoc, 2, 16, bgcolor=color.new(#131722, 8), border_width=1, frame_width=1, frame_color=color.new(color.gray, 50))

    color okCol   = color.new(color.green, 80)
    color warnCol = color.new(color.orange, 70)
    color badCol  = color.new(color.red, 70)
    color headCol = color.new(color.gray, 75)
    color offCol  = color.new(color.gray, 85)

    string stateMsg = not envOk ? "BLOCKED" : state == 3 ? "IN TRADE" : state == 2 ? "ORDER WORKING" : state == 1 and bosDone ? "BROKEN - GATES" : state == 1 ? "SWEPT - AWAITING BREAK" : not htfOk ? "NO 4H RANGE" : "WAITING FOR SWEEP"
    color stateCol = not envOk ? badCol : state >= 2 ? okCol : state == 1 ? warnCol : offCol

    table.cell(dash, 0, 0, "TP 4H/15m Fib", text_color=color.white, bgcolor=headCol, text_size=size.small)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 0, stateMsg, text_color=color.white, bgcolor=stateCol, text_size=size.small)

    string envMsg = not htfHigher ? "HTF <= chart TF" : not atrOk ? "ATR warming up" : "OK"
    table.cell(dash, 0, 1, "Chart", text_color=color.silver, text_size=size.tiny)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 1, envMsg, text_color=envOk ? color.lime : color.red, text_size=size.tiny)

    // Split into where price sits and what that permits, because "above range" and
    // "premium" are not the same statement and the run that made this necessary
    // showed 193% — price a full range width above a stale high, read as premium.
    string biasWhere = not htfOk ? "no range" : aboveRng ? "ABOVE range" : not inRange ? "BELOW range" : premium ? "premium" : discount ? "discount" : "equilibrium"
    string biasAllow = allowShort and allowLong ? "both" : allowShort ? "sells only" : allowLong ? "buys only" : "no trade"
    table.cell(dash, 0, 2, htfTf + " bias", text_color=color.silver, text_size=size.tiny)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 2, biasWhere + " - " + biasAllow + (na(htfPos) ? "" : "  " + str.tostring(htfPos, "#") + "%"), text_color=not inRange and htfOk ? color.orange : allowShort or allowLong ? color.lime : color.orange, text_size=size.tiny)

    table.cell(dash, 0, 3, htfTf + " range", text_color=color.silver, text_size=size.tiny)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 3, htfOk ? str.tostring(htfLo, format.mintick) + " - " + str.tostring(htfHi, format.mintick) : "n/a", text_color=color.white, text_size=size.tiny)

    string sweepMsg = state == 0 ? "none" : (dir == -1 ? "buy side -> short" : "sell side -> long") + (na(sweepBar) ? "" : "  (" + str.tostring(bar_index - sweepBar) + " bars)")
    table.cell(dash, 0, 4, "Sweep", text_color=color.silver, text_size=size.tiny)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 4, sweepMsg, text_color=state == 0 ? color.silver : color.orange, text_size=size.tiny)

    string bosMsg = state == 0 ? "n/a" : bosDone ? "broken @ " + str.tostring(bosLvl, format.mintick) : "needs " + (dir == -1 ? "close < " : "close > ") + str.tostring(bosRefLvl, format.mintick)
    table.cell(dash, 0, 5, "Structure", text_color=color.silver, text_size=size.tiny)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 5, bosMsg, text_color=bosDone ? color.lime : color.silver, text_size=size.tiny)

    string fvgMsg = state == 0 ? "n/a" : str.tostring(array.size(gapTop)) + " in leg" + (aPlus ? "   A+ 71% inside" : "")
    table.cell(dash, 0, 6, "Imbalance", text_color=color.silver, text_size=size.tiny)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 6, fvgMsg, text_color=aPlus ? color.lime : array.size(gapTop) > 0 ? color.white : color.silver, text_size=size.tiny)

    string legMsg = na(legStart) or na(legEnd) ? "n/a" : str.tostring(legStart, format.mintick) + " -> " + str.tostring(legEnd, format.mintick) + "  (" + str.tostring(legRange / syminfo.mintick, "#") + " pts)"
    table.cell(dash, 0, 7, "Fib leg 100->0", text_color=color.silver, text_size=size.tiny)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 7, legMsg, text_color=color.white, text_size=size.tiny)

    // The stop in ticks sits next to the entry because it is the number that decides
    // whether the trade is economic, and it is invisible in the price alone.
    string entryMsg = na(wantEntry) ? "n/a" : str.tostring(wantEntry, format.mintick) + (na(riskDist) ? "" : "   stop " + str.tostring(riskDist / syminfo.mintick, "#") + "t" + (costOk ? "" : " TOO SMALL"))
    table.cell(dash, 0, 8, str.tostring(retrPct, "#") + "% entry", text_color=color.silver, text_size=size.tiny)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 8, entryMsg, text_color=costOk ? color.aqua : color.orange, text_size=size.tiny)

    string gateMsg = state == 0 ? "waiting" : not bosDone ? "no break of structure" : not biasOk ? (inRange ? "4H bias blocks it" : "price outside 4H range") : not fvgOk ? "no imbalance" : not alignOk ? "71% not in imbalance" : not rrOk ? "RR too low" : not costOk ? "stop too small vs cost" : not retrOk ? "limit through market" : not coolOk ? "cooldown" : not sessOk ? "outside session" : qty <= 0 ? "qty 0" : "clear"
    table.cell(dash, 0, 9, "Gates", text_color=color.silver, text_size=size.tiny)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 9, gateMsg, text_color=gateMsg == "clear" ? color.lime : gateMsg == "waiting" ? color.silver : color.orange, text_size=size.tiny)

    table.cell(dash, 0, 10, "Live setup RR", text_color=color.silver, text_size=size.tiny)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 10, na(setupRR) ? "n/a" : str.tostring(setupRR, "#.##") + "  (need " + str.tostring(minRR, "#.##") + ")", text_color=rrOk ? color.lime : color.orange, text_size=size.tiny)

    table.cell(dash, 0, 11, "Last E / SL / TP", text_color=color.silver, text_size=size.tiny)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 11, na(lastEntry) ? "none yet" : str.tostring(lastEntry, format.mintick) + " / " + str.tostring(lastStop, format.mintick) + " / " + str.tostring(lastTgt, format.mintick) + "  @" + str.tostring(lastRR, "#.##") + "R", text_color=color.white, text_size=size.tiny)

    table.cell(dash, 0, 12, "Last qty", text_color=color.silver, text_size=size.tiny)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 12, na(lastQty) ? "none yet" : str.tostring(lastQty, "#.##") + (lastCap ? "  (capped)" : ""), text_color=lastCap ? color.orange : color.white, text_size=size.tiny)

    // A leg stuck at zero is the failure mode that reads as a bad strategy: it
    // means one direction's condition is contradicting itself and half the model
    // has never run. The Strategy Tester cannot show you this.
    table.cell(dash, 0, 13, "Orders S / L", text_color=color.silver, text_size=size.tiny)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 13, str.tostring(nShort) + " / " + str.tostring(nLong), text_color=nShort == 0 or nLong == 0 ? color.orange : color.white, text_size=size.tiny)

    // The funnel. Read it left to right and stop at the first big drop — that is
    // the stage rejecting your setups, and the only one worth changing a setting for.
    string funnel = str.tostring(nSweep) + " > " + str.tostring(nBos) + " > " + str.tostring(nArmed) + " > " + str.tostring(nFilled)
    color funCol = nSweep == 0 ? color.silver : nFilled == 0 ? color.orange : color.white
    table.cell(dash, 0, 14, "Swp>BOS>Ord>Fill", text_color=color.silver, text_size=size.tiny)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 14, funnel, text_color=funCol, text_size=size.tiny)

    float beWin  = na(minRR) or minRR <= 0 ? na : 100.0 / (1.0 + minRR)
    float aRate  = nFilled > 0 ? nAplus * 100.0 / nFilled : na
    table.cell(dash, 0, 15, "Cancel/Time  A+%", text_color=color.silver, text_size=size.tiny)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 15, str.tostring(nCancel) + " / " + str.tostring(nTime) + "   A+ " + (na(aRate) ? "-" : str.tostring(aRate, "#") + "%") + "   BE win " + str.tostring(beWin, "#.#") + "%", text_color=color.white, text_size=size.tiny)
